    "id": "2012hawaiiintermediatecourtofappealsstatevloebel",
    "judge_ruling": " The issue in State v Loebel H I A AP 34956 June 8 2012 rev'g HSC 27 2012 was whether a defendant had standing to seek discovery from the prosecution based on prior bad acts arising out of an ongoing investigation that would have been discoverable during trial proceedings A deputy prosecutor in the Department of Health and Human Services Criminal Investigation Division DHS CID discovered evidence after receiving a tip about a woman who worked for the organization but was arrested by DHS before she could be located She had allegedly engaged in prostitution with an undercover officer while working as a nurse at one facility under investigation on charges connected to another case involving the same employer The defendant was charged and convicted based largely upon her testimony during this arrest The State requested discovery of various documents from the investigator who made the initial tip the deputy prosecutor at that time or any other law enforcement officer involved with those prior proceedings \u2014 a request rejected by the trial court as unreasonable under Hawaii Rule of Evidence 104 b The appellate Court held the defendant had standing to challenge this discovery refusal on two independent grounds: first because evidence discovered during DHS's investigation was discoverable and second",
